ACE: Not bad...
Tyrone: Woah...
Tyrone: I'm wondering how it works right now...
ACE: Let me show you what Java did behind the scenes.
ACE: It started at main and read your line. System.out.println told it to print something.
ACE: Then it took whatever sat between the quotation marks, exactly as it was, and sent it to the screen.
Tyrone: So the words in quotes are the message itself.
ACE: Exactly! And the semicolon told Java that this instruction was finished.
Tyrone: What if I forget it?
Tyrone: (I probably forgot it at least five times or less...)
ACE: Then Java stops with an error before printing anything. Computers are very literal.
ACE: That's why those slips are worth learning from. One missing character can break the whole program.
Tyrone: Quotes for the message, semicolon to end the line. Got it.
Tyrone: I have this gut thought this actually gets harder the further it goes...
ACE: At least you're learning, right?
Tyrone: ...
Tyrone: ...yeah, I guess so.
Tyrone: Hopefully I couldn't forget this too.
